---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ACTSATConcordance from '../components/ACTSATConcordance';

const pageTitle = "SAT Score Equivalent to ACT - Official Score Comparison";
const pageDescription = "Find your ACT score equivalent based on your SAT total. Use official College Board concordance tables to compare your SAT and ACT scores.";
const pageKeywords = "SAT score equivalent to ACT, SAT to ACT conversion, convert SAT to ACT, what is my SAT score in ACT";

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: "SAT Score Equivalent to ACT", item: "/sat-score-equivalent-act" }
];

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is the SAT score equivalent the same as the ACT score?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. The SAT uses a scale of 400-1600, while the ACT uses 1-36. They test similar skills, but the scores are not one-to-one. You must use a concordance table to find the equivalent."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do colleges compare SAT and ACT?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Admissions officers use the official ACT/SAT concordance tables to compare applicants. If you submit an SAT score, they will look at its ACT equivalent to see how you stack up against ACT test-takers."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Should I take both tests?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "It can be beneficial to take a practice test for both to see which format you prefer. If you score significantly higher on one, focus your studying on that test."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} keywords={pageKeywords} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />
  </Fragment>
  <main class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-10 sm:py-14">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <div class="text-center mb-10">
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-black text-slate-900 tracking-tight font-heading mb-4">
        SAT Score Equivalent to ACT
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-slate-600 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        Many colleges accept both test scores. Use this tool to find out what your <strong>SAT score equivalent to ACT</strong> is on the 36-point scale.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Concordance Component (Default: SAT to ACT) -->
    <div class="mb-16 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <ACTSATConcordance client:load defaultDirection="sat-to-act" />
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2>Why Convert Scores?</h2>
      <p>
        If you have taken both the SAT and the ACT, you should submit the score that is relatively higher. But since the scoring scales are completely different (400-1600 vs. 1-36), you need an official conversion chart to compare them.
      </p>

      <h3>How Colleges View Equivalent Scores</h3>
      <p>
        Admissions officers use these exact concordance tables to standardize applicant data. An SAT score of <strong>1400</strong> is treated as roughly equal to an ACT score of <strong>30</strong>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Sending Scores:</strong> You generally do not need to send both scores unless a specific program requests it. Send your strongest one.</li>
        <li><strong>Merit Aid:</strong> Some scholarships have specific cutoffs for each test (e.g., "1350 SAT or 29 ACT"). Always check the conversion to see if you qualify.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Conversion Chart (SAT → ACT)</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-slate-800 text-white">
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left rounded-tl-lg">SAT Range</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left rounded-tr-lg">ACT Composite</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1570–1600</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">36</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1530–1560</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">35</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1490–1520</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">34</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1450–1480</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">33</td>
            </tr>
             <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
               <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1420–1440</td>
               <td class="px-4 py-3">32</td>
             </tr>
             <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
               <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1390–1410</td>
               <td class="px-4 py-3">31</td>
             </tr>
             <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
               <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1360–1380</td>
               <td class="px-4 py-3">30</td>
             </tr>
             <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
               <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1330–1350</td>
               <td class="px-4 py-3">29</td>
             </tr>
             <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
               <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1300–1320</td>
               <td class="px-4 py-3">28</td>
             </tr>
             <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
               <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1260–1290</td>
               <td class="px-4 py-3">27</td>
             </tr>
             <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
               <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1230–1250</td>
               <td class="px-4 py-3">26</td>
             </tr>
             <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
               <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1200–1220</td>
               <td class="px-4 py-3">25</td>
             </tr>
             <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
               <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1160–1190</td>
               <td class="px-4 py-3">24</td>
             </tr>
             <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
               <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1130–1150</td>
               <td class="px-4 py-3">23</td>
             </tr>
             <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
               <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1100–1120</td>
               <td class="px-4 py-3">22</td>
             </tr>
             <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
               <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1060–1090</td>
               <td class="px-4 py-3">21</td>
             </tr>
             <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
               <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1020–1050</td>
               <td class="px-4 py-3">20</td>
             </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">
        Based on the official 2018 ACT/SAT concordance published jointly by the College Board and ACT, Inc. Ranges are rounded for readability; use the interactive tool above for a single-point lookup.
      </p>

      <h2>How the SAT and ACT Differ</h2>
      <p>
        The two exams cover overlapping skills but package them very differently. Understanding the structural differences explains why a direct point-for-point conversion isn't possible and why the concordance table exists in the first place.
      </p>
      <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse bg-white shadow-sm rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-800 text-white">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Feature</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">SAT</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">ACT</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100 border border-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Total score scale</td><td class="px-4 py-3">400–1600</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1–36 (composite)</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-slate-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Sections</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Reading &amp; Writing, Math</td><td class="px-4 py-3">English, Math, Reading, Science*</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Format</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Digital, adaptive</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Digital or paper, linear</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-slate-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Pace</td><td class="px-4 py-3">More time per question</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Faster, less time per question</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Calculator</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Allowed on all math</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Allowed on all math</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">*As of 2025, the ACT Science section is optional. See our <a href="/act-score-calculator-without-science" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">ACT calculator without science</a>.</p>

      <h2>Worked Examples: Reading Your SAT in ACT Terms</h2>
      <p>
        Concordance becomes intuitive once you see it applied. Here are three common SAT totals translated to the ACT scale and what that equivalence means in practice:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>1500 SAT ≈ 34 ACT:</strong> Both sit around the 99th percentile. Either score is competitive for the most selective universities — there is no advantage to retaking.</li>
        <li><strong>1350 SAT ≈ 29 ACT:</strong> A strong, top-10% score. If a scholarship lists "30 ACT" as its cutoff, you're one point short on the ACT scale even though your SAT is excellent — worth knowing before you apply.</li>
        <li><strong>1200 SAT ≈ 25 ACT:</strong> Above the national average on both tests. Solid for many state universities and a reasonable baseline to build on with further prep.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Strategies for High Scorers</h2>
      <p>
        If your SAT score is already in the 1500+ range, sitting the ACT can act as a "second chance" at a perfect score. Because the tests reward slightly different skills, some students who plateau near the top of the SAT scale find a clean 36 ACT easier to reach — and a 36 looks just as strong on an application as a 1600. Before committing to a retake, confirm the concordance: a 1540 SAT already equals a 35 ACT, so the upside may be a single point.
      </p>

      <h2>When the Conversion Actually Matters</h2>
      <p>
        For most applicants, you simply submit whichever score is higher on the concordance and move on. The conversion matters most in three situations: (1) <strong>scholarship cutoffs</strong> that are stated in only one test's units, (2) <strong>NCAA or honors-program eligibility</strong> thresholds, and (3) deciding <strong>which test to focus your remaining prep on</strong>. If you want the reverse direction, see our <a href="/act-score-equivalent-sat" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">ACT score equivalent to SAT</a> page, or get the full picture with the <a href="/sat-act-score-converter" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">SAT/ACT concordance converter</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Other SAT &amp; ACT Conversion Tools</h2>
      <p>
        This page breaks down an SAT total into ACT terms at the section level. For a different need, use one of our companion resources:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/sat-act-score-converter">SAT to ACT Converter</a> &mdash; the full interactive concordance plus a "which test is right for you" breakdown.</li>
        <li><a href="/act-sat-score-converter">ACT to SAT Converter</a> &mdash; ACT-first tool with target scores by college tier and ACT superscoring rules.</li>
        <li><a href="/act-score-equivalent-sat">ACT Score Equivalent to SAT</a> &mdash; percentile matching and scholarship-cutoff guidance for an ACT composite.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="not-prose space-y-4">
        <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Is the SAT score equivalent the same as the ACT score?</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">
             No. The SAT uses a scale of 400-1600, while the ACT uses 1-36. They test similar skills, but the scores are not one-to-one. You must use a concordance table to find the equivalent.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">How do colleges compare SAT and ACT?</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">
            Admissions officers use the official ACT/SAT concordance tables to compare applicants. If you submit an SAT score, they will look at its ACT equivalent to see how you stack up against ACT test-takers.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Should I take both tests?</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">
            It can be beneficial to take a practice test for both to see which format you prefer. If you score significantly higher on one, focus your studying on that test.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
